last_report.behaviour_change:
The T1 and T2E exponential fit (`a exp(-t/T) + c`, function `fit_exp_decay`) is now bounded and always returns a result instead of raising. T is limited to [dx/2, 20 x span], where dx is the median step and span is the sweep length. |a| is limited to 10 x the peak-to-peak of the trace, c is free, maxfev=10000, and the start values are the old ones clipped into the bounds. On well-behaved decays the fitted T matches the old unbounded fit to about 1e-5 relative; this is checked in the tests. On noisy data the old fit could run away to T ~ 1e6 us; now T stops at 20 x span. The function returns a dict {ok, T, T_err, a, c, popt, perr, yfit, T_bounds, error}. `ok` is False, with a readable reason in `error`, in any of these cases:
- the optimiser fails;
- the input is degenerate (fewer than 4 points, non-finite, constant y, no x spread, or a length mismatch);
- T ends on a bound;
- the covariance is not finite;
- T_err >= T;
- |a| < 3 a_err, meaning no significant decay.

On pure noise about 0.3% of fits still come out ok (about 6% without the |a| check). T1FF.display and T2EMUX.display wrap the fit in try/except. On an exception they print the traceback and set `self.fit = {'ok': False, 'error': repr(e)}`. They never raise for a fit reason, and they still write both PNGs. If the fit is ok, the plot title shows T +- err as before. If not, the title says "T1/T2E fit not reliable" and any fitted curve is drawn dashed grey. The fit is saved in the h5: display writes float fields into data['data'], and finish() saves them because it runs display first. The T1 keys are t1_fit_ok (1/0), t1_fit_us, t1_fit_err_us, t1_fit_a, t1_fit_c, t1_fit_popt [a, T, c], t1_fit_perr and t1_fit_rotation_rad (the IQ rotation actually used). T2E uses the same keys with the t2e_ prefix. A field with no value is NaN. The full dict is kept on the instance as `.fit`. The tProc programs, cfg and acquisition are unchanged.
